# Nomey - environment variables template
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values.
# `.env` is git-ignored and must NEVER be committed.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY - read before adding anything here
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every variable prefixed with EXPO_PUBLIC_ is INLINED INTO THE APP BUNDLE and is
# readable by anyone who downloads the .ipa/.apk.
#
# THE INVARIANT: a backend credential with elevated privileges is never present
# in the client bundle. No exception, no variant, no "just for testing".
#
# Supabase publishable keys (sb_publishable_...) are documented as safe to embed
# in a mobile app, so EXPO_PUBLIC_ is the right prefix for them. Row access is
# still governed by RLS and grants on the server side.
#
# Secret keys (sb_secret_...) are backend-only: Edge Functions, servers, CI.
# They belong in GitHub Secrets / Supabase secrets, never here.
#
#   FORBIDDEN:  EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=...
#   FORBIDDEN:  EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=...
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API KEY SYSTEM
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nomey uses Supabase's current API keys: publishable for the client, secret for
# backend components. The legacy `anon` / `service_role` JWT keys are documented
# by Supabase as deprecated, so a new project should not start on them.
#
# Legacy names are recorded here only so older tutorials can be translated:
#     anon          ~ publishable (client)
#     service_role  ~ secret      (backend)
#
# Treat that as a rough map, not an equivalence. The new keys differ in
# implementation and compatibility, particularly around Edge Functions and JWT
# verification. Whenever a specific integration depends on a key's
# authentication behaviour, verify it against the current official docs before
# implementing.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTRACT - three variables, and nothing else
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nomey's whole environment surface is three names. ADR-031 fixes it, and
# `tests/infra/env-contract.test.ts` fails if a fourth appears.
#
#   APP_VARIANT                            build-time; NOT in this file, see below
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL               public client configuration
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY   public client configuration
#
# Neither EXPO_PUBLIC_ value is a secret. They are public configuration that is
# designed to travel in the binary, and calling them secrets would be the kind
# of imprecision that ends with someone treating a real secret as if it were one
# of them.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APP_VARIANT - do NOT set it here
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `APP_VARIANT` selects which of the three identities is being built:
#
#     development   Nomey Dev       es.lcworks.nomey.dev       nomey-dev
#     staging       Nomey Staging   es.lcworks.nomey.staging   nomey-staging
#     production    Nomey           es.lcworks.nomey           nomey
#
# It belongs to the COMMAND, not to this file:
#
#     npm start                  ->  development, explicitly
#     npm run config:staging     ->  staging
#     node scripts/with-variant.mjs staging export
#
# Putting it in `.env` would make the identity of a build depend on a file that
# is not in Git, so two machines running the same command could produce two
# different apps. Leaving it unset resolves to `development`; a value that is
# not one of the three fails loudly. Production is never implicit.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THERE IS NO EXAMPLE VALUE FOR THE KEY
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `tests/infra/no-backend-secrets.test.ts` forbids any publishable-key literal
# in versioned source, this file included, so that nobody can paste the real one
# here "just as an example". A placeholder shaped like a key would either defeat
# that guard or force an exception to it, and the guard is worth more than the
# convenience. The shape is described instead:
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL               an http(s) URL with a host. For the
#                                          local stack, use the loopback address
#                                          `http://127.0.0.1:54321` and let ADB
#                                          bridge it to the device - see below.
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY   starts with `sb_publishable_`. A
#                                          `sb_secret_` value or any JWT is
#                                          rejected at startup by
#                                          `src/lib/env/supabase-env.ts`.
#
# Both are empty below on purpose: an empty value fails immediately and
# unmistakably, which is the correct behaviour for a template.
#
# ON THE URL, AND WHY IT IS NOT A LAN ADDRESS. It used to be one, and that made
# the file depend on where you were sitting: a LAN address stops resolving the
# moment you join another Wi-Fi, and the symptom does not show up at build time
# but inside the app, as a network failure far from its cause. The fix was to
# edit this file on every move.
#
# Use `http://127.0.0.1:54321` instead and let ADB bridge the port into the
# device:
#
#   npm run android              # opens the tunnels, then starts Metro
#   npm run android:reverse:check   # verifies them without touching anything
#
# `10.0.2.2` would also reach the host, but only from the emulator: a phone on a
# cable does not resolve it. Loopback plus `adb reverse` serves both, and needs
# no open ports and no firewall change. Details in
# `docs/runbooks/android-build.md` section 6.

EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
